Tension Pneumothorax
Acute Urgent Care
Absent breath sounds, Hyperresonant percussion, Distended neck veins, Hypotension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-048
Reason for Visit: Extreme difficulty breathing, turning blue, and near-collapse following a high-impact fall from a ladder.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who fell 3 meters from a ladder onto their side, immediately developing severe, gasping respiratory distress and a rapid loss of consciousness. The presentation of extreme air hunger combined with hemodynamic collapse post-thoracic trauma is the hallmark of a tension pneumothorax. Urgent, on-the-spot clinical assessment and immediate life-saving needle decompression were absolutely critical, as waiting for a standard ambulance response would result in fatal obstructive shock and cardiac arrest within minutes.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Neighbor reports] He fell off the roof, and now he is gasping for air, clutching his chest, and his lips are completely blue!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with severe acute respiratory distress following a high-energy blunt trauma (3-meter fall from a ladder onto the right side) 15 minutes ago. The patient exhibits profound tachypnoea and extreme air hunger, unable to speak in sentences. The neighbor notes the patient has rapidly deteriorated, becoming highly confused, agitated, and is now slipping into unconsciousness. The patient complains of a crushing, sharp pain on the right side of the chest that makes it impossible to inhale.
Functional Status: Lying supine on the grass, highly agitated, gasping.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of traumatic cardiac arrest.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 75/40 | HR: 138 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 36 | SpO2: 82% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient is in extremis, deeply cyanotic, profoundly diaphoretic, and struggling violently to breathe.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals asymmetric chest wall movement with visibly decreased expansion on the right side. Palpation identifies severe subcutaneous emphysema spreading across the right chest wall into the neck. Percussion yields a hyperresonant, drum-like note over the right hemithorax. Auscultation demonstrates completely absent breath sounds on the right side. Cardiovascular exam reveals hemodynamic instability presenting as profound hypotension and compensatory tachycardia (obstructive shock). Inspection of the neck reveals markedly distended jugular veins despite the hypotension. Tracheal deviation to the left (away from the affected side) is palpable. Expected investigations (post-decompression) would include an anteroposterior chest radiograph demonstrating complete right lung collapse with contralateral mediastinal shift.
Environmental Observations: Ladder lying on the ground; area cleared for immediate medical intervention.
Medication Reconciliation: Unknown, irrelevant in the acute trauma resuscitation phase.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension Pneumothorax - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Massive Haemothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a hyperresonant percussion note (rather than dull) and distended neck veins (rather than flat), indicating trapped pressurized air rather than massive blood loss.

Simple Rib Fractures - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of profound obstructive shock, distended neck veins, and tracheal deviation, which indicate a severe mechanical shift of the mediastinum, not just bone pain.

Cardiac Tamponade - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clear unilateral absence of breath sounds and hyperresonance, pointing directly to a pulmonary/pleural pressure issue rather than a primary pericardial fluid collection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the lethal diagnostic tetrad of a tension pneumothorax: absent breath sounds, hyperresonant percussion, distended neck veins, and profound hypotension. Following blunt trauma, a "one-way valve" has formed, trapping pressurized air in the right pleural cavity, collapsing the lung, and physically crushing the heart and great vessels (mediastinal shift), causing obstructive shock. The urgent after-hours intervention was unequivocally life-saving. Immediate needle thoracostomy was required to convert the tension pneumothorax into a simple pneumothorax, instantly restoring venous return to the heart and preventing impending traumatic cardiac arrest.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate needle thoracostomy (decompression) using a large-bore (14G) intravenous cannula inserted into the 2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line on the right side. An immediate rush of air was heard. The patient's respiratory distress rapidly improved, SpO2 rose to 94%, and BP improved to 105/65 within 2 minutes. Simultaneously activated 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch. Applied high-flow oxygen.
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A - Critical trauma stabilization).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Helicopter Emergency Medical Service (HEMS) or rapid road transport to a Level 1 Trauma Center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to trauma team for formal intercostal catheter (chest tube) insertion.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Continuous monitoring of the patient's breathing and consciousness. If the patient's condition rapidly deteriorates again (indicating the cannula has kinked or clotted), a second needle decompression will be performed immediately adjacent to the first.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You have suffered a massive chest injury where air was trapped around your lung, crushing your heart. I have placed a needle to let the air out, which has saved your life.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will replace this small needle with a larger plastic tube to keep the lung fully inflated while the hole in your lung heals over the next few days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to sit up or move. Take slow, steady breaths. Do not touch the needle in your chest under any circumstances.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fall tore your lung, allowing air to escape into your chest cavity but not get back out. The pressure was so high it stopped your heart from beating properly. The needle I inserted acted as an escape valve to release that pressure instantly.
Actionable Steps: Try to remain as calm as possible. The specialized intensive care paramedics are arriving now to safely transport you to the trauma surgeons.

